{"id": "b2a2f62f-cad1-476f-8b49-a7b50ea27a44", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T18:25:26.711927", "source": "user", "parent_id": "0eaa4c3d-65a6-4e3e-b712-9e284f5899bd", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Approve. The structure is solid — I like that you isolated the non-causal aspect with condition C rather than just doing A vs B, and the random-kernel ablation is the right sanity check. The multi-scale ACF + banded PSD combo directly answers the 'multiple timescales' part of the question rather than hiding behind a single FID number. Good. Now go write the full design. A few things to lock in as you write it up: one, since I've got no clarification topics available to you, the rollout protocol question you flagged becomes a stated assumption in the design. My read on SiT is that it's a diffusion/denoising framework, so the non-causal variant most plausibly does full-sequence denoising over a fixed generation window rather than step-by-step autoregressive rollout. State that as your working assumption, note the alternative (chunked bidirectional rollout with overlap), and specify which metrics would differ under each protocol so the reader can see the sensitivity. Two, for 'extremely long' — 1k to 32k steps is fine as the primary range, but add a sensitivity note: if the claim is about 100k+ steps, what would change in the design (training window length, evaluation compute, whether the PSD bands need to extend further). Don't over-engineer it, just one paragraph. Three, the matched-parameter control is a hard requirement, not a suggestion — make that explicit in a dedicated 'Controls and Confounds' section. Call out that attention and implicit long convolution have different effective memory footprints even at matched param counts, and say how you'd handle that (e.g., report both param-matched and compute-matched comparisons if feasible, or at minimum state the effective receptive field difference). Four, feasibility section: since this is design-only and the environment is CPU-only with no ML deps, frame feasibility as 'what would a lab with N GPUs need to run this' — give a rough FLOP/VRAM estimate per condition, total wall-clock, and what you'd cut first if budget is tight (I'd say cut condition E and drop to 2 seeds before cutting the multi-scale analysis). Five, the synthetic multi-scale probe: specify it concretely. I'm thinking something like a two-frequency coupled oscillator or a stochastic process with a known fast mode (period ~10 steps) and a slow mode (period ~500-1000 steps), so you can decompose which timescale degrades first. Make the ground truth computable analytically so you don't need a separate 'reference model.' Six, structure the final doc as: Question and Hypothesis, Experimental Conditions (A-E with exact specs), Operational Definitions (extremely long, consistency, multi-scale), Analysis Plan (primary and secondary metrics, statistical tests, effect sizes), Controls and Confounds, Feasibility and Resource Estimate, Assumptions and Limitations, Expected Outcomes and Decision Criteria (what result would support vs. refute the hypothesis). Keep the rationale tight — I don't want a 20-page thesis, I want a design doc a collaborator could pick up and execute in a week. Go.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
